{:edb/db-before-t 2219 :edb/db-after-t 2220 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535532 50 #inst "2026-10-14T08:57:29.219Z" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186054673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512938 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186068810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512892 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186078403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512867 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186087692 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512891 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186087692 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512917 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186087856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512889 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186098349 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098351 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186100862 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512922 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186100862 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512950 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186100863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512919 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186100863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512947 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186100863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512953 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186100953 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512873 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186100962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512879 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186101169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186101169 1071 "![](images/a4/a4a238887e789979e276421850330c5b36a82c485312c06e831c0df96266b85b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 1, 5]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6$. Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 1, 5]$ given that $f(-1) = 1$." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186101169 1071 "![](images/a4/a4a238887e789979e276421850330c5b36a82c485312c06e831c0df96266b85b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5]$ given that $f(-1)=1.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186101169 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =1 \\\\ f(5) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,7\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186119753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512902 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186119892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512898 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512936 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186133763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512895 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186133763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512906 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186133764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512865 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186133780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512878 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186154804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512182 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186154804 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512182 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186154807 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512488 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186154807 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512488 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157044 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^2 + e^{-2x}$ in the interval $[0,1]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.5$. Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186157044 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^2 + e^{-2x}$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157044 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$, respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (x^{2} + e^{-2x})^{′} \\\\\n&= 2x - 2e^{-2x}, \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= (2x - 2e^{-2x})^{′} \\\\\n&= 2 + 4e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\nx_{n + 1} = x_{n} - \\frac{2x_{n} - 2e^{-2x_{n}}}{2 + 4e^{-2x_{n}}}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\frac{2x_{0} - 2e^{-2x_{0}}}{2 + 4e^{-2x_{0}}} \\\\\n&= 0.5 - (2(0.5) - 2e^{-2(0.5)})/(2 + 4e^{-2(0.5)}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.424\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186157044 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}+{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =2x-2{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x-2{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =2+4{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}-2{e}^{-2{x}_{n}}}{2+4{e}^{-2{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}-2{e}^{-2{x}_{0}}}{2+4{e}^{-2{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{2(0.5)-2{e}^{-2(0.5)}}{2+4{e}^{-2(0.5)}} \\\\  & ≈0.424,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157045 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^3-6x^2+9x$ in the interval $[0,2]$. Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0$." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186157045 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^3-6x^2+9x$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157045 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$, respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (x^{3} - 6x^{2} + 9x)^{′} = 3x^{2} - 12x + 9, \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= (3x^{2} - 12x + 9)^{′} = 6x - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{3x_{n}^{2} - 12x_{n} + 9}{6x_{n} - 12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\frac{3x_{0}^{2} - 12x_{0} + 9}{6x_{0} - 12} \\\\\n&= 0 - (3(0)^{2} - 12(0) + 9)/(6(0) - 12) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{12} \\\\\n&= 0.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\frac{3x_{1}^{2} - 12x_{1} + 9}{6x_{1} - 12} \\\\\n&= 0.75 - (3(0.75)^{2} - 12(0.75) + 9)/(6(0.75) - 12) \\\\\n&= 0.975\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x_1 = 0.75$ and\n\n$$\nx_2 = 0.975\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186157045 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-12x+9, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2}-12x+9)^{′}=6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{2}-12{x}_{n}+9}{6{x}_{n}-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{2}-12{x}_{0}+9}{6{x}_{0}-12} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3(0)^{2}-12(0)+9}{6(0)-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12} \\\\  & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{2}-12{x}_{1}+9}{6{x}_{1}-12} \\\\  & =0.75-\\frac{3(0.75)^{2}-12(0.75)+9}{6(0.75)-12} \\\\  & =0.975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x_1 = 0.75$ and $x_2 = 0.975.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157046 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)=x^4-3x+1$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,2]$, use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186157046 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)=x^4-3x+1$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157046 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$, respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (x^{4} - 3x + 1)^{′} = 4x^{3} - 3, \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= (4x^{3} - 3)^{′} = 12x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - \\frac{4x_{n}^{3} - 3}{12x_{n}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (12x_{n}^{3} - (4x_{n}^{3} - 3))/(12x_{n}^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8x_{n}^{3} + 3}{12x_{n}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= \\frac{8x_{0}^{3} + 3}{12x_{0}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (8(1)^{3} + 3)/(12(1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 0.916666…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= \\frac{8x_{1}^{3} + 3}{12x_{1}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (8(0.916666…)^{3} + 3)/(12(0.916666…)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 0.908631…, \\\\\nx_{3} &= \\frac{8x_{2}^{3} + 3}{12x_{2}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (8(0.908631…)^{3} + 3)/(12(0.908631…)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 0.908560…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations\n\n$$\nx_2 \\approx0.91\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_3 \\approx 0.91\n$$\n\nare the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.91\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186157046 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3x+1)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-3, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{3}-3)^{′}=12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-3}{12{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{x}_{n}^{3}-(4{x}_{n}^{3}-3)}{12{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}_{n}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{8{x}_{0}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(1)^{3}+3}{12(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.916\\,666….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{8{x}_{1}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(0.916\\,666…)^{3}+3}{12(0.916\\,666…)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.908\\,631…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{8{x}_{2}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(0.908\\,631…)^{3}+3}{12(0.908\\,631…)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.908\\,560….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations $x_2 \\approx0.91$ and $x_3 \\approx 0.91$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x \\approx 0.91,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512874 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512896 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512916 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157426 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512925 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512870 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512894 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512940 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512956 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512863 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512884 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512924 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512935 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512948 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157519 1050 1500.0 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157713 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x) = x^{3} + x^{2} - 3x + 4$ in the interval $[0, 2]$. Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point $x_{0} = 0$. Round your answer to three decimal places.$x_{2} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186157713 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3x+4$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157713 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3x+4)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+2x-3, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2}+2x-3)^{′}=6x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{2}+2{x}_{n}-3}{6{x}_{n}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{2}+2{x}_{0}-3}{6{x}_{0}+2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3(0)^{2}+2(0)-3}{6(0)+2} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-3}{6{x}_{1}+2} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{3(1.5)^{2}+2(1.5)-3}{6(1.5)+2} \\\\  & ≈0.886,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157716 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x) = \\sin (x^{2}) + x^{3}$ in the interval $[- 2, 0]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_{0} =-1$. Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.$x_{1} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186157716 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+{x}^{3}$  in the interval $[-2,0].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=-1.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186157716 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+{x}^{3})^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})+3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})+3{x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})+3{x}_{n}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-4{x}_{n}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})+3{x}_{0}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-4{x}_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{2(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2})+3(-1)^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2})-4(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2})+6(-1)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-3}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-6} \\\\  & ≈-0.768,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186158355 1061 459710 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186158355 1061 688822 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186158356 1061 459711 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186158356 1061 688823 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186158357 1061 459712 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186158357 1061 688824 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186158358 1061 459715 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186158358 1061 688827 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186158359 1061 459718 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186158359 1061 688830 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186158438 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to *find the roots* of a function using *Newton’s method*. That is, we solved equations of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nby starting with an initial guess and refining it step by step using the update rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nNow, we turn to a closely related problem of *finding a local minimum or maximum* of a function $f(x)$. Recall that these may occur at points where the derivative $f'(x)$ is equal to zero. In other words, we are looking for the roots of the function's derivative.\n\nEarlier, we approximated the function by a straight line near our current guess. Here, instead of using a linear approximation, we assume that the function $f(x)$ behaves like a *parabola* near our current guess. That’s a reasonable assumption if $f$ is smooth.\n\nTo make this idea precise, we use a second-order Taylor approximation, $F(x){:}$\n\n$$\nF(x) = f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n) + \\dfrac{1}{2} f''(x_n)(x - x_n)^2\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum or maximum, we first need to take the derivative of this approximation, which is\n\n$$\nF'(x) = f'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n)\n$$\n\nWe then need to set $F'(x) =0$:\n\n$$\nf'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n) = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x_{n})(x - x_{n}) &= -f^{′}(x_{n}) \\\\\nx - x_{n} &= - (f^{′}(x_{n}))/(f^{″}(x_{n})) \\\\\nx &= x_{n} - (f^{′}(x_{n}))/(f^{″}(x_{n}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, just like in root-finding, we start with an initial guess and improve it step by step. But this time, we apply Newton’s method to the derivative $f'(x)$ instead of the function $f(x)$ itself:\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\n**Note**: The second derivative $f''(x_n)$ must not be zero. If it is zero, or even very close to zero, the update step can become extremely large, leading us far away from the solution.\n\nJust like in root-finding, Newton’s method works best when the function is smooth, and our initial guess is already close to the minimum or maximum." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186158438 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to *find the roots* of a function using *Newton’s method*. That is, we solved equations of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0,\n$$\n\nby starting with an initial guess and refining it step by step using the update rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we turn to a closely related problem of *finding a local minimum or maximum* of a function $f(x)$. Recall that these may occur at points where the derivative $f'(x)$ is equal to zero. In other words, we are looking for the roots of the function's derivative.\n\nEarlier, we approximated the function by a straight line near our current guess. Here, instead of using a linear approximation, we assume that the function $f(x)$ behaves like a *parabola* near our current guess. That’s a reasonable assumption if $f$ is smooth. \n\nTo make this idea precise, we use a second-order Taylor approximation, $F(x){:}$\n\n$$\nF(x) = f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n) + \\dfrac{1}{2} f''(x_n)(x - x_n)^2\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum or maximum, we first need to take the derivative of this approximation, which is\n\n$$\nF'(x) = f'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n).\n$$\n\nWe then need to set $F'(x) =0$:\n\n$$\nf'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n) = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}({x}_{n})(x-{x}_{n}) & =-{f}^{′}({x}_{n}) \\\\ x-{x}_{n} & =-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})} \\\\ x & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, just like in root-finding, we start with an initial guess and improve it step by step. But this time, we apply Newton’s method to the derivative $f'(x)$ instead of the function $f(x)$ itself:\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\n**Note**: The second derivative $f''(x_n)$ must not be zero. If it is zero, or even very close to zero, the update step can become extremely large, leading us far away from the solution.\n\nJust like in root-finding, Newton’s method works best when the function is smooth, and our initial guess is already close to the minimum or maximum." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186158457 1064 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\ln(x+1)+\\dfrac{2}{x+1}\n$$\n\nin the interval $[0,2]$. Let's calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.8$.\n\nThe Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n$, respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (\\ln (x + 1) + \\frac{2}{x + 1})^{′} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x + 1} - (2)/((x + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (x - 1)/((x + 1)^{2}), \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= (\\frac{1}{x + 1} - (2)/((x + 1)^{2}))^{′} \\\\\n&= - (1)/((x + 1)^{2}) + (4)/((x + 1)^{3}) \\\\\n&= (3 - x)/((x + 1)^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{n + 1} &= x_{n} - ((x_{n} - 1)/((x_{n} + 1)^{2}))/((3 - x_{n})/((x_{n} + 1)^{3})) \\\\\n&= x_{n} - \\frac{x_{n}^{2} - 1}{3 - x_{n}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.8$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\frac{x_{0}^{2} - 1}{3 - x_{0}} \\\\\n&= 0.8 - ((0.8)^{2} - 1)/(3 - 0.8) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.964\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186158457 1064 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\ln(x+1)+\\dfrac{2}{x+1}\n$$\n\nin the interval $[0,2].$ Let's calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.8.$ \n\nThe Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{2}{x+1})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{4}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-x}{(x+1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\frac{{x}_{n}-1}{({x}_{n}+1)^{2}}}{\\frac{3-{x}_{n}}{({x}_{n}+1)^{3}}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}-1}{3-{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{x}_{0}^{2}-1}{3-{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{(0.8)^{2}-1}{3-0.8} \\\\  & ≈0.964,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186159320 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159321 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186159320 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159322 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186159320 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159323 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186159320 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159324 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186159320 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159325 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186159320 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512157 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186159320 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512160 13194139535532 17592186045417 true