nal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$. These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.1$ | $0.9$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y]$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 0.6 + 2 \\cdot 0.4 \\\\\n&= 0.6 + 0.8 \\\\\n&= 1.4 \\\\\nE[Y] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{Y})y \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.1 + 1 \\cdot 0.9 \\\\\n&= 0 + 0.9 \\\\\n&= 0.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)(x - E[X])(y - E[Y])f(x, y) \\\\\n&= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)(x - 1.4)(y - 0.9)f(x, y) \\\\\n&= (1 - 1.4)(0 - 0.9)f(1, 0) \\\\\n&= + (1 - 1.4)(1 - 0.9)f(1, 1) \\\\\n&= + (2 - 1.4)(0 - 0.9)f(2, 0) \\\\\n&= + (2 - 1.4)(1 - 0.9)f(2, 1) \\\\\n&= (-0.4)(-0.9)(0.1) \\\\\n&= + (-0.4)(0.1)(0.5) \\\\\n&= + (0.6)(-0.9)(0) \\\\\n&= + (0.6)(0.1)(0.4) \\\\\n&= 0.04\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161698 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.1$ | $0.9$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.6+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.6+0.8 \\\\  & =1.4 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.9 \\\\  & =0+0.9 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.4)(y-0.9)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1-1.4)(0-0.9)f(1,0) \\\\  & +(1-1.4)(1-0.9)f(1,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.4)(0-0.9)f(2,0) \\\\  & +(2-1.4)(1-0.9)f(2,1) \\\\  & =(-0.4)(-0.9)(0.1) \\\\  & +(-0.4)(0.1)(0.5) \\\\  & +(0.6)(-0.9)(0) \\\\  & +(0.6)(0.1)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161700 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/be/bebcce16140d945e7f9059627d76fc4a28f5638294029c4ccf3ccd0adbfb71a2.svg)\n\nFind the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161700 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n${\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x}y,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 2 \\le y \\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind the covariance of $X$ and $Y$." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161700 1074 "The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [2,3]\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = g(x) \\cdot h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ng(x) =\\dfrac{3}{5} \\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand $h(y) =y$.\n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161700 1074 "The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle \n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [2,3].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = g(x) \\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x) =\\dfrac{3}{5} \\sqrt{x}$ and $h(y) =y.$ \n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161701 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S=S_X\\times S_Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.45$ | $0.15$ |\n\nFind an expression for the covariance in sigma notation." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161701 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S=S_X\\times S_Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.45$ | $0.15$ |\n\nFind an expression for the covariance in sigma notation." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161701 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x - \\textrm E[X])(y - \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$. These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.45$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y]$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 0.4 + 4 \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 0.4 + 2.4 \\\\\n&= 2.8 \\\\\nE[Y] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{Y})y \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 0.6 + 5 \\cdot 0.4 \\\\\n&= 1.2 + 2 \\\\\n&= 3.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)(x - E[X])(y - E[Y])f(x, y) \\\\\n&= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)(x - 2.8)(y - 3.2)f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161701 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.45$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.4+2.4 \\\\  & =2.8 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.6+5\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.2+2 \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2.8)(y-3.2)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161702 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $\\textrm{E} [X]=2.8$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.4,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161702 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $\\textrm{E} [X]=2.8$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.4$, calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y$." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161702 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n=\n\\textrm{E} [XY] - \\textrm{E}[X]\\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\textrm{E}[XY]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(2)(0.2)+(1)(3)(0.2)+(4)(2)(0.4)+(4)(3)(0.2) \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =6.6-(2.8)(2.4) \\\\  & =-0.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161702 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E} [XY] - \\textrm{E}[X]\\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\textrm{E}[XY]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)xy \\cdot f(x, y) \\\\\n&= (1)(2)(0.2) + (1)(3)(0.2) + (4)(2)(0.4) + (4)(3)(0.2) \\\\\n&= 6.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 6.6 - (2.8)(2.4) \\\\\n&=-0.12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161907 1071 "![](images/d1/d1fd70958028ae2c12be1df5808616421da9bb5758999237031a143e48d8e167.svg)\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161907 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X$." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161907 1074 "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{1}{4}(x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3} + x_{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161907 1074 "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n![](images/f9/f97a4bc5d9323be573cc78cca322502d86d4c890d46b7116ffa1fac87e291672.svg)\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161908 1071 "![](images/44/44fa46a2d63c5c59dcc95df4132bdfa56400989234b44d2a382eaa2e51d5713b.svg)\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186161908 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186161908 1074 "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\fr