{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186162422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186162422 1071 "If $E[X] = 20$, $E[Y] = 2$, and $E[XY] = 44.8$ for two random variables $X$ and $Y$, calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186162422 1071 "If $\\text{E}[X]=20,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=2,$ and $\\text{E}[XY]=44.8$ for two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186162422 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =44.8-20\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of $X$ and $Y$ is $4.8.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186162429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186162429 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x>0,\\,y>0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186162429 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$f(x, y) = {e^{-x - y},\\begin{vmatrix}x > 0, y > 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind the covariance of $X$ and $Y$." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186162429 1074 "The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle \n\n$$\nS=(0,\\infty )\\times (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)={e}^{-x}$ and $h(y)={e}^{-y}.$ \n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186162445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186162445 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S = S_{X} \\cdot S_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X = 3$ | $0.28$ | $0.2$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186162445 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.28$ | $0.2$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186162445 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ | $0.52$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.28$ | $0.2$ | $0.48$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.52+3\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =0.52+1.44 \\\\  & =1.96 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.4+5\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.8+3 \\\\  & =3.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.96)(y-3.8)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186163290 1064 "We often want to measure the strength of the (linear) dependence between two random variables. One measure that we use for this purpose is covariance.\n\nThe **covariance** between two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\textrm{E}\\Big[\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right) \\Big]\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- $\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)$ is the deviation of $X$ from its mean\n- $\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right)$ is the deviation of $Y$ from its mean\n\nTherefore, the covariance measures the expected value of the product of these deviations.\n\nThe covariance can also be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x - \\textrm E[X])(y - \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of $X$ and $Y$, and $S$ is the joint support.\n\nLet's sketch a few diagrams to get a feel for what the covariance tells us.\n\n- A *positive* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to increase. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **positively correlated**.![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n- A *negative* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to decrease. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **negatively correlated**.![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n$\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = 0$.\nThe reverse implication does not always hold! For example, two random variables can have zero covariance yet be dependent. This is because covariance measures *linear* dependence only.\n- The covariance of a random variable with itself is its variance:\n$Cov[X, X]|= E[(X - E[X])(X - E[X])]; = E[(X - E[X])^{2}]; = Var[X]$\n\n- Aside from indicating whether two random variables are positively or negatively correlated, the covariance *by itself* does not tell us very much. For example, if\n$\\textrm{Cov}[X_1, Y_1] > \\textrm{Cov}[X_2, Y_2] > 0$\nthis does not necessarily mean that the dependence between $X_1$ and $Y_1$ is somehow \"stronger\" than the dependence between $X_2$ and $Y_2$. This is because the covariance does not take the variance of the individual random variables into account. In a future lesson, we'll use the covariance to define the so-called **correlation coefficient,** which *does* consider the variance of the individual random variables and so *can* be used to compare the relative strength of the linear dependence between two pairs of random variables." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186163290 1064 "We often want to measure the strength of the (linear) dependence between two random variables. One measure that we use for this purpose is covariance. \n\nThe **covariance** between two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\textrm{E}\\Big[\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right) \\Big].\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- $\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)$ is the deviation of $X$ from its mean\n\n- $\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right)$ is the deviation of $Y$ from its mean\n\nTherefore, the covariance measures the expected value of the product of these deviations.\n\nThe covariance can also be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of $X$ and $Y,$ and $S$ is the joint support.\n\nLet's sketch a few diagrams to get a feel for what the covariance tells us.\n\n- A *positive* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to increase. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **positively correlated**.\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A *negative* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to decrease. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **negatively correlated**.\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = 0.\n$$\n\nThe reverse implication does not always hold! For example, two random variables can have zero covariance yet be dependent. This is because covariance measures *linear* dependence only.\n\n- The covariance of a random variable with itself is its variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,X] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])(X-\\text{E}[X])] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{(X-\\text{E}[X])}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Aside from indicating whether two random variables are positively or negatively correlated, the covariance *by itself* does not tell us very much. For example, if \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_1, Y_1] > \\textrm{Cov}[X_2, Y_2] > 0\n$$\n\nthis does not necessarily mean that the dependence between $X_1$ and $Y_1$ is somehow \"stronger\" than the dependence between $X_2$ and $Y_2.$ This is because the covariance does not take the variance of the individual random variables into account. \n\nIn a future lesson, we'll use the covariance to define the so-called  **correlation coefficient,** which *does* consider the variance of the individual random variables and so *can* be used to compare the relative strength of the linear dependence between two pairs of random variables." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186163292 1064 "Let's now prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] - \\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of covariance, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E\\Big[\\left(X- \\textrm E[X]\\right)\\left(Y-\\textrm E[Y]\\right)\\Big]\n$$\n\nExpanding the product in the brackets, we get\n\n$$\nCov[X, Y] = E[XY - X \\cdot E[Y] - E[X] \\cdot Y + E[X] \\cdot E[Y]]\n$$\n\nNow, recall that if $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[a]=a, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X] + b \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nSince $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and are $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ are both constants, the covariance formula simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY - X \\cdot E[Y] - E[X] \\cdot Y + E[X] \\cdot E[Y]] \\\\\n&= E[XY] - E[X \\cdot E[Y]] - E[E[X] \\cdot Y] + E[E[X] \\cdot E[Y]] \\\\\n&= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] + E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186163292 1064 "Let's now prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] − \\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of covariance, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm  E\\Big[\\left(X− \\textrm E[X]\\right)\\left(Y−\\textrm E[Y]\\right)\\Big].\n$$\n\nExpanding the product in the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY-X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that if $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[a]=a, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X] + b \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSince $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and are $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ are both constants, the covariance formula simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY-X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]]-\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y]+\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186163293 1064 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, the covariance between $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\iint_\\limits{\\mathbb R^2}(x - \\textrm E[X])(y - \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y) \\, \\textrm d x\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nAs is the case with discrete random variables, it's often easier to compute the covariance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E} [XY] - \\textrm{E}[X]\\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nSuppose we're given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{2}(x + \\frac{y}{2}),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTo compute the covariance of this distribution, we first use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\textrm{E}[XY]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= \\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}xyf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}xy \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x + \\frac{y}{2})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y\\int_{0}^{1}(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}xy)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y[\\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + \\frac{1}{4}x^{2}y]_{x = 0}^{x = 1}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y[\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4}y]dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}y + \\frac{1}{8}y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{12}y^{2} + \\frac{1}{24}y^{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 2^{2} + \\frac{1}{24} \\cdot 2^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing methods discussed in previous lessons, we can compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12}, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac76\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} - (\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{6}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{72}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that if two random variables are independent then their covariance equals zero. Let's see an example." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186163293 1064 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, the covariance between $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\iint_\\limits{\\mathbb R^2}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y) \\, \\textrm d x\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nAs is the case with discrete random variables, it's often easier to compute the covariance using the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n=\n\\textrm{E} [XY] - \\textrm{E}[X]\\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nSuppose we're given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/c5/c589b62f579cb5efe7c4a2b86aa9bebbbbec81517c8bd7872c3c6a0b019e08cc.svg)\n\nTo compute the covariance of this distribution, we first use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\textrm{E}[XY]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x+\\frac{y}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}y+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing methods discussed in previous lessons, we can compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y]{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12}, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac76\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that if two random variables are independent then their covariance equals zero. Let's see an example." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186163310 1064 "The table below describes the number of classes taken per semester and the average final grade in a sample of four college students.\n\n| **Number of Courses** | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Average Grade** | $80$ | $85$ | $75$ | $72$ |\n\nWe can collect this data in a matrix so that each column reports the number of courses and the average grade of a single student:\n\n![](images/8a/8a489e90c10dbb65c2f5e005c32677696073cc63203116ed07d445a91d7f2d32.svg)\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **observation matrix.**\n\nSuppose we want to determine the mean number of courses and the mean average grade of this sample. The **sample mean vector,** denoted $\\mathbf m,$ is a vector containing these values. The sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom our matrix above, we have that  \n\n![](images/45/4549af29f9356865dc9f3edb67960da00a4007a71281778d4a273a1a6eb0dbf9.svg)\n\nTherefore, the sample mean vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 80\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 85\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 75\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 312\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the average number of courses is $4,$ and the average final grade is $78.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186163310 1064 "The table below describes the number of classes taken per semester and the average final grade in a sample of four college students.\n\n| **Number of Courses** | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Average Grade** | $80$ | $85$ | $75$ | $72$ |\n\nWe can collect this data in a matrix so that each column reports the number of courses and the average grade of a single student:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 80 & 85 & 75 & 72\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **observation matrix.**\n\nSuppose we want to determine the mean number of courses and the mean average grade of this sample. The **sample mean vector,** denoted $\\mathbf m$, is a vector containing these values. The sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom our matrix above, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{1}{4}(x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3} + x_{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 85\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 75\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 312\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the average number of courses is $4$, and the average final grade is $78$." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186163311 1064 "Let's consider again the mean-deviation matrix for the students' data we found previously:\n\n![](images/1f/1f5440b7ae08ffd19bdae816854c236afe3ca254d5bd23e26f15e34cd9c9feb2.svg)\n\nCalculating the product $B B^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 98\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's analyze what we've obtained:\n\n- The entry in the first row and first column (${\\color{red}2}$) is the sum of the squares of the mean deviations of the number of courses.\n\n- The entry in the second row and second column (${\\color{blue}98}$) is the sum of the squares of the mean deviations of the final grades.\n\n- The off-diagonal entries (${\\color{purple}-10}$) are the sum of the products of the mean deviations.\n\nSince there were $4$ observations in total, if we divide each entry by $4-1=3,$  we will get a matrix $C$ with the *sample variances* in the diagonal and the *sample covariance* in the off-diagonal positions:\n\n![](images/7d/7d0216b462e1bf7dc34d638a7a0de30fcf063ce39d1865b45f85b526ba7bd0a8.svg)\n\nThis matrix is called the **covariance matrix** of the data.\n\nIn general, the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186163311 1064 "Let's consider again the mean-deviation matrix for the students' data we found previously:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nCalculating the product $B B^T$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 98\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's analyze what we've obtained:\n\n- The entry in the first row and first column (${\\color{red}2}$) is the sum of the squares of the mean deviations of the number of courses.\n- The entry in the second row and second column (${\\color{blue}98}$) is the sum of the squares of the mean deviations of the final grades.\n- The off-diagonal entries (${\\color{purple}-10}$) are the sum of the products of the mean deviations.\n\nSince there were $4$ observations in total, if we divide each entry by $4-1=3$, we will get a matrix $C$ with the *sample variances* in the diagonal and the *sample covariance* in the off-diagonal positions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 98\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis matrix is called the **covariance matrix** of the data.\n\nIn general, the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186163321 1064 "Earlier, we studied data that reported the number of courses selected and the average grades of a sample of four college students. The observation matrix for this data was\n\n![](images/9b/9bdfe848443d0014461cc51e2decfbed8dc151a72edb34825dd33ac964ed6b2f.svg)\n\nThe individual observations are\n\n![](images/3f/3f935ba5f9adea7f75a63642837a3b993286f9535d5bf1842ce3a2267413c6bc.svg)\n\nWe also found the sample mean vector \n\n![](images/26/261e1d746aafa95ac2f7a6d1ff36a741ff5ae37ba738f75b451a542baed1c5fc.svg)\n\nOne thing we can do with this data is to check how much each observation differs from the mean values. To do that, we compute the difference between each observation and the sample mean vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 80\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 85\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 75\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{4} & ={x}_{4}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe collect these differences in a new matrix, as follows:\n\n![](images/83/831e698a632d40ab447c75a3b169e29c6ae8ab10c6600d838bd699a38e837931.svg)\n\nThe matrix $B$ is the **mean-deviation form** of $X.$ It's often easier to work with the mean-deviation form, especially if the original data contains large numbers.\n\nNotice that the mean of a mean-deviation form equals the zero vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{B} & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186163321 1064 "Earlier, we studied data that reported the number of courses selected and the average grades of a sample of four college students. The observation matrix for this data was\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 80 & 85 & 75 & 72\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe individual observations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe also found the sample mean vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nOne thing we can do with this data is to check how much each observation differs from the mean values. To do that, we compute the difference between each observation and the sample mean vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{1} &= x_{1} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{2} &= x_{2} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 85\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{3} &= x_{3} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 75\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{4} &= x_{4} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe collect these differences in a new matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $B$ is the **mean-deviation form** of $X$. It's often easier to work with the mean-deviation form, especially if the original data contains large numbers.\n\nNotice that the mean of a mean-deviation form equals the zero vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{B} &= \\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186163343 1064 "You may have noticed that computing the covariance using the definition is quite a lot of work. Is there a formula that makes it easier?\n\nIndeed, there is. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] - \\textrm E [X]\\cdot \\textrm E [Y]\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying it." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186163343 1064 "You may have noticed that computing the covariance using the definition is quite a lot of work. Is there a formula that makes it easier?  \n\nIndeed, there is. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] − \\textrm E [X]\\cdot \\textrm E [Y].\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying it." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186166931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512949 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186166932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512886 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186166932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512957 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186169167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169168 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186169167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186169169 13194139535532 1759218604541