$ |" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186188560 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20} & ∼N(20\\cdot 1,\\,20\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(20\\cdot 1,\\,20\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(20,80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\le 21).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 21) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{21-20}{\\sqrt{80}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{80}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.11) \\\\  & =Φ(0.11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.11)=0.5438.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 21)=Φ(0.11)=0.5438.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186188679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186188679 1071 "A nationwide study found that $38%$ of families own a pet. If a town randomly samples $200$ families, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $80$ of them are pet owners. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186188679 1071 "A nationwide study found that $38%$ of families own a pet. If a town randomly samples $200$ families, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $80$ of them are pet owners. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186188679 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=200,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.38.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=200(0.38)=76>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=200(1-0.38)=124>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=200$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,\\frac{0.38(1-0.38)}{200}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,0.0012).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $80$ families in the sample own a pet, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}>\\frac{80}{200}=0.4.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.38}{\\sqrt{0.0012}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.58) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.58) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186198352 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512908 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186202599 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512520 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186202599 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512520 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186202600 1075 #edb/ref 17592186202601 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186202600 1075 #edb/ref 17592186202602 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186202600 1075 #edb/ref 17592186202603 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186202600 1075 #edb/ref