a ,$ and the angle complementary to $\\theta +{62}^{∘}$ is\n\n$$\n{90}^{∘}-(\\theta +{62}^{∘})={90}^{∘}-\\theta -{62}^{∘}={28}^{∘}-\\theta .\n$$\n\n So we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta +{62}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}-(\\theta +{62}^{∘}))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({28}^{∘}-\\theta ).\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512766 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512773 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233306 1071 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}0+\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233306 1071 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233306 1074 "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}0=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}0=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}0}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}0+\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1+1=2.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233306 1074 "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1+1=2.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512732 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233424 1071 "Which of the following expressions are **irrational**?\n\nI. ${8}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}$\n\nII. ${3}^{20}\\cdot 7.0523$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {2}^{-15}$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233424 1071 "Which of the following expressions are **irrational**?\n\nI. - ${8}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}$\nII. - ${3}^{20}\\cdot 7.0523$\nIII. - $\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {2}^{-15}$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512816 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233432 1071 "![](images/ce/cec25c6ab182494771824bae8c2182bf38b57e2d1e81d913e3e49916c8cc3d60.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - When $x=8,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\nII. - The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\nIII. - This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233432 1071 "![](images/ce/cec25c6ab182494771824bae8c2182bf38b57e2d1e81d913e3e49916c8cc3d60.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. When $x=8,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\n\nII. The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\nIII. This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233432 1074 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=8$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates  this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0bb186e7b1c1923e7e8b2776b202b1dcd9b930c56583199964e47530805d246d.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233432 1074 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=8$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates  this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512862 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233460 1071 "Given the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following transformations in the order given:\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- Translate it to the left by $10$ units\n\nWhat is the final function expression after applying all the transformations?" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233460 1071 "Given the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following transformations in the order given:\n\n1\\. Stretch it by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n2\\. Stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n3\\. Translate it to the left by $10$ units\n\nWhat is the final function expression after applying all the transformations?" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233460 1074 "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Stretching the graph of $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $8.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n$$\ny=8f(x).\n$$\n\n- Stretching the graph of $y=8f(x)$ by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $\\frac{1}{5}x.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n$$\ny=8f(\\frac{1}{5}x)=8f(\\frac{x}{5}).\n$$\n\n- Translating the graph of $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5})$ by $10$ units to the left is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $(x+10).$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n$$\ny=8f(\\frac{1}{5}(x+10))=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233460 1074 "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n1\\. Stretching the graph of $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $8.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(x).\n  $$\n\n2\\. Stretching the graph of $y=8f(x)$ by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $\\frac{1}{5}x.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}x)=8f(\\frac{x}{5}).\n  $$\n\n3\\. Translating the graph of $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5})$ by $10$ units to the left is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $(x+10).$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}(x+10))=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).\n  $$\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512854 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512727 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512759 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233592 1071 "![](images/a6/a61a6232b2041bab3d455a7d765311ddfae25f674f8dbbf79696d2137355b159.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram shown above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space.\n\nGiven that every outcome is equally likely, find $P({A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'}).$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233592 1071 "![](images/a6/a61a6232b2041bab3d455a7d765311ddfae25f674f8dbbf79696d2137355b159.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram shown above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space.\n\nGiven that every outcome is equally likely, find $P({A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′}).$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233592 1074 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, ${A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'}$ is represented by the area outside the circle $B$ or outside the circle $A$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/6a/6a3bb7b671e45aeb15d3239cae6fcedb7c36e52b15141d66b23e65066ab37edc.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in ${A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'}$ is \n\n$$\n2+5+1=8\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n2+9+5+1=17.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}^{'}\\cup {B}^{'})=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233592 1074 "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, ${A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′}$ is represented by the area outside the circle $B$ or outside the circle $A$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/6a/6a3bb7b671e45aeb15d3239cae6fcedb7c36e52b15141d66b23e65066ab37edc.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in ${A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\n2+5+1=8\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n2+9+5+1=17.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}^{′}\\cup {B}^{′})=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233682 1071 "Consider the frequency table below for a continuous variable $x.$ Assuming that the observations within each class interval are uniformly distributed, the total number of observations is $n=20,$ and the estimated sample mean is $\\overset{―}{x}\\approx 13.5.$\n\n| Range | Frequency $({f}_{i})$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $8$ |\n| $10\\le x<20$ | $7$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $5$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nBased on these distributional assumptions, find the standard estimate for the sample standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{n}.$\n\nRounded to one decimal place, ${\\sigma }_{n}\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233682 1071 "Consider the frequency table below for a continuous variable $x.$ The total number of observations is $n=20,$ and the estimated sample mean is $\\overset{―}{x}≈13.5.$\n\n| Range | Frequency $({f}_{i})$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $8$ |\n| $10\\le x<20$ | $7$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $5$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nUsing the midpoint of each class interval to approximate all observations within that class, estimate the standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{n},$ using $n$ as the denominator in the variance formula.\n\nRounded to one decimal place, ${\\sigma }_{n}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233682 1074 "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, the variance can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{n}^{2}\\approx \\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{K}{f}_{i}\\cdot ({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${f}_{i}$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n\n- ${m}_{i}$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class,\n\n- $n$ is the total number of observations, and\n\n- $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean.\n\nTo find the variance, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each interval, the squared deviations of the midpoints from the mean, and the products of the squared deviations and frequencies.\n\n| Range | ${f}_{i}$ | ${m}_{i}$ | ${({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ | ${f}_{i}{({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $8$ | $5$ | $72.25$ | $578$ |\n| $10\\le x<20$ | $7$ | $15$ | $2.25$ | $15.75$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $5$ | $25$ | $132.25$ | $661.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, we divide the sum of all the values in the last column by $20$ (the total number of observations):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n}^{2} & \\approx \\frac{578+15.75+661.25}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1255}{20} \\\\  & =62.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n} & =\\sqrt{62.75}\\approx 7.9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233682 1074 "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, we can estimate the variance by treating all observations within each class as though they were equal to the midpoint of that class. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{n}^{2}≈\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{K}{f}_{i}\\cdot ({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${f}_{i}$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n\n- ${m}_{i}$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class,\n\n- $n$ is the total number of observations, and\n\n- $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the estimated mean, calculated using the class midpoints.\n\nTo find the variance, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each interval, the squared deviations of the midpoints from the mean, and the products of the squared deviations and frequencies.\n\n| Range | ${f}_{i}$ | ${m}_{i}$ | ${({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ | ${f}_{i}{({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $8$ | $5$ | $72.25$ | $578$ |\n| $10\\le x<20$ | $7$ | $15$ | $2.25$ | $15.75$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $5$ | $25$ | $132.25$ | $661.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, we divide the sum of all the values in the last column by $20$ (the total number of observations):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n}^{2} & ≈\\frac{578+15.75+661.25}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1255}{20} \\\\  & =62.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the estimated standard deviation is the square root of the estimated variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n} & ≈\\sqrt{62.75}≈7.9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512843 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512803 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233937 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{'}$ the angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{'}+\\theta =2\\pi $\n\n![](images/eb/eb659af0765e200333ca35b716772d92765c7f46ccc4b267243b3c82ba88a1e8.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}{\\theta }^{'}=\\frac{6}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{'}=\\operatorname{arctan}\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta +{\\theta }^{'}=2\\pi ,$ we get $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186233937 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+\\theta =2\\pi $\n\n![](images/eb/eb659af0765e200333ca35b716772d92765c7f46ccc4b267243b3c82ba88a1e8.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{6}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta +{\\theta }^{′}=2\\pi ,$ we get $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512791 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512840 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234060 1071 "Which of the following transformations map every polygon to a congruent polygon?\n\nI. $(x,y)↦(x-1,y)$\n\nII. $(x,y)↦(-y,x)$\n\nIII. $(x,y)↦(2x,y)$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234060 1071 "Which of the following transformations map every polygon to a congruent polygon?\n\nI. - $(x,y)↦(x-1,y)$\nII. - $(x,y)↦(-y,x)$\nIII. - $(x,y)↦(2x,y)$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512849 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512742 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512740 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234105 1074 "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the park $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{'}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the park changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the park at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{'}(t)=E(t)-L(t).\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234105 1074 "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of people inside the park $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of people inside the park changes $t$ minutes after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of people inside the park at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t).\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512831 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512764 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234141 1071 "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\nI. - Stretching with a stretch factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction and a stretch factor of $4$ in the $y$-direction\nII. - Translation by $3$ units to the left and $4$ units upward\nIII. - Dilation of scale factor $2$ with center $O,$ then reflection across the $y$-axis" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234141 1071 "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\nI. Stretching with a stretch factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction and a stretch factor of $4$ in the $y$-direction\n\nII. Translation by $3$ units to the left and $4$ units upward\n\nIII. Dilation of scale factor $2$ with center $O,$ then reflection across the $y$-axis" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512804 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512760 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234168 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ in terms of a reference angle." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234168 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ in terms of a reference angle." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234168 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={126}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is equivalent to ${126}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/89/89b5e2656378adb5d91c9f58c261e4beaae252b67819b904bf209f6ed8a5a25e.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n- Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n- Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n- Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})=-\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}).\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234168 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })={126}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is equivalent to ${126}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/89/89b5e2656378adb5d91c9f58c261e4beaae252b67819b904bf209f6ed8a5a25e.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{10})=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}).\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512836 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512823 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512832 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512750 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234280 1074 "Let $f(x)=6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{1/2}+5x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{-1/2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(1)=3(1)^{-1/2}+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =8(x-1) \\\\ y & =8x-8-2 \\\\ y & =8x-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =8x-10 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(\\frac{5}{4},0).$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234280 1074 "Let $f(x)=6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\sqrt{x}+5x-13) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{1/2}+5x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{-1/2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{-1/2}+5=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =8(x-1) \\\\ y & =8x-8-2 \\\\ y & =8x-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =8x-10 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(\\frac{5}{4},0).$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512814 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234325 1071 "![](images/38/38e096b8d57404135b7fe3244e50989e66021f94bb2275c56956d8663575b4f8.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234325 1071 "![](images/4c/4ce20d9d732816efda0a2fd3aebabc467f9f2c73d0b6037f4ad8f95d8e8ef1e7.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234325 1074 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/6e/6e070d26915bcb11118f5a23f36b80038d5dd865e32a8517a12c10939ca02f0b.png)\n\nFirst, we find the area of $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{85}^{∘} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{85}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈23.908\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area of $△BCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BCD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{36.4}^{∘} \\\\  & =112\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{36.4}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈66.462\\,9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BCD} \\\\  & ≈23.908\\,7+66.462\\,9 \\\\  & ≈90.371\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $90.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234325 1074 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 23.908\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & =112\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 67.403\\,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =23.908\\,7+67.403\\,3 \\\\  & =91.311\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $91.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512844 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512796 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512726 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512858 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234488 1071 "Calculate ${log}_{8}(32)$ without using a calculator." 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234488 1071 "Calculate ${log}_{8}⁡(32)$ without using a calculator." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234488 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ and $X=32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}(32) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}(32)}{{log}_{2}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}({2}^{5})}{{log}_{2}({2}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234488 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8,$ and $X=32.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2,$ we set $b=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡(32) & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(32)}{{log}_{2}⁡(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5})}{{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512829 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186234512 1074 "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{16}^{1/4}=2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${16}^{1/4}=2$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=2,b=16$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${log}_{16}2=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 false] [17592186234512 1074 "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{16}^{1/4}=2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${16}^{1/4}=2$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=2,b=16$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${log}_{16}⁡2=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186317451 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A=0 \\\\ \\pi B=6\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186317451 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $