f 17592186045493 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512860 1077 "selection" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512860 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512205 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512860 1079 #uuid "f815c63a-5ecf-5ce3-b831-33cde02c9d6b" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512183 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512430 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512479 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512482 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512861 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512688 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512861 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512861 1077 "selection" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512861 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512482 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512861 1079 #uuid "fe515d97-b46e-5b25-875a-5d4729fa788a" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512537 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512567 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512595 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512664 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512716 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512862 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512862 1077 "selection" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512862 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512567 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512862 1079 #uuid "ffa36a73-cb38-5a3e-b22f-94ae7972e4d0" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512850 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512863 1069 "q-367495" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512863 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+2$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.65$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512863 1072 #uuid "003098de-0500-522c-8022-2eac5e6b41e0" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512863 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12x)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}-12)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n})}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.65,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.65)^{4}}{20(1.65)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.428\\,269…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.428\\,269…)^{4}}{20(1.428\\,269…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.349\\,003…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}}{20{x}_{2}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.349\\,003…)^{4}}{20(1.349\\,003…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.339\\,016…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{15{x}_{3}^{4}}{20{x}_{3}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.339\\,016…)^{4}}{20(1.339\\,016…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.338\\,865….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.34$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.34$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.34,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512825 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512864 1069 "q-138593" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512864 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 7 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512864 1072 #uuid "02ad0f7a-4664-5f54-8474-68fa73e23e7a" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512864 1074 "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512817 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512865 1069 "q-76450" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512865 1071 "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ].$ Find $⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512865 1072 #uuid "04de5946-03c2-52be-a91c-cc43fa9abf34" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512865 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )}{6}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512860 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512866 1069 "q-251865" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512866 1071 "Consider the two sets of $6$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $123$ | $122$ | $121$ | $120$ | $125$ | $127$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $102$ | $111$ | $110$ | $105$ | $106$ | $108$ |\n\nCalculate ${S}_{xy}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512866 1072 #uuid "06d00ab5-b798-54f9-9155-cb3b190eccdd" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512866 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their respective means, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sample means $\\overset{―}{x}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{123+122+121+120+125+127}{6}=123\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{102+111+110+105+106+108}{6}=107\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the differences between the ${x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{x}$, ${y}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}$, and their product.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $123$ | $122$ | $121$ | $120$ | $125$ | $127$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $102$ | $111$ | $110$ | $105$ | $106$ | $108$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\,$** | $0$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| **$\\,({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})\\,$** | $-5$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})\\,$** | $0$ | $-4$ | $-6$ | $6$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, ${S}_{xy}$ is\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=0+(-4)+(-6)+6+(-2)+4=-2.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512824 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512867 1069 "q-35003" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512867 1071 "![](images/06/06e792c5b74eda036b55ac3728122737da22c9824f89f982b50657ab07391269.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512867 1072 #uuid "0821d791-2798-561e-8237-7b4aabdd155c" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512867 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x),$ and hence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\n We can't say that the limits are equal because we can't compare undefined values.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512781 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512868 1069 "q-251840" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512868 1071 "Consider the two sets of $6$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $22$ | $24$ | $25$ | $23$ | $21$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $13$ | $15$ | $16$ | $18$ | $14$ | $17$ |\n\nCalculate ${S}_{yy}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512868 1072 #uuid "0adf3af5-290b-57ec-82ed-01a980659e66" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512868 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ ${S}_{yy}$ is the sum of the squared deviations of the data ${y}_{i}$ from the mean $\\overset{―}{y},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sample mean $\\overset{―}{y}:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{13+15+16+18+14+17}{6}=15.5\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the differences between the ${y}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}$, and their squares.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $22$ | $24$ | $25$ | $23$ | $21$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $13$ | $15$ | $16$ | $18$ | $14$ | $17$ |\n| **$\\,({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})\\,$** | $-2.5$ | $-0.5$ | $0.5$ | $2.5$ | $-1.5$ | $1.5$ |\n| **$\\,({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\\,$** | $6.25$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ | $6.25$ | $2.25$ | $2.25$ |\n\nTherefore, ${S}_{yy}$ is\n\n$$\n{S}_{yy}=6.25+0.25+0.25+6.25+2.25+2.25=17.5.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512751 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512869 1069 "q-147919" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512869 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.35$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512869 1072 #uuid "0b02c01d-81c9-557d-8302-9b53cb371ef3" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512869 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.35$ | $0.4$ | $0.75$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.75+4\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =1.5+1 \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.4+1.2 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2.5)(y-1.6)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512754 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512790 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512870 1069 "q-256860" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512870 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})-4x$ in the interval $[0.2,1].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=0.2.$ Round your answers to three decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512870 1072 #uuid "0cd7384c-d991-5262-b3c3-37190574fa1c" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512870 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})-4x)^{′}=\\frac{2}{x}-4, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{2}{x}-4)}^{′}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\frac{2}{{x}_{n}}-4}{-\\frac{2}{{x}_{n}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}+{x}_{n}(1-2{x}_{n}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{n}(1-{x}_{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{0}(1-{x}_{0}) \\\\  & =2(0.2)(1-0.2) \\\\  & =0.32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{x}_{1}(1-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & =2(0.32)(1-0.32) \\\\  & =0.4352\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}=0.32$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.435,$ rounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512761 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512871 1069 "q-147211" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512871 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $70%$ but less than $80%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9599\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9608\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9671\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9678\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9686\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9738\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9744\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9750\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3300\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3669\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3632\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4013\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3974\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512871 1072 #uuid "102d618f-6dbe-5b03-8427-52afbec9d15d" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512871 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.72.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.72)=69.12>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.72)=26.88>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,\\frac{0.72(1-0.72)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,0.0021).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.7-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.44<Z\\le 1.75) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.75)-P(Z\\le -0.44) \\\\  & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.75) & =0.9599 \\\\ Φ(-0.44) & =0.3300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44) \\\\  & =0.9599-0.3300 \\\\  & =0.6299.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512812 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512872 1069 "q-138628" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512872 1071 "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512872 1072 #uuid "11c41377-dcf0-57a2-b4f3-049053a2f2ed" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512872 1074 "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & 15 \\\\ 15 & 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512746 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512873 1069 "q-100106" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512873 1071 "![](images/51/51b119021be889ff440ec31d901187c3ee9072a507336a8e1a1854f729f2734f.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512873 1072 #uuid "124cf999-fb4b-5e99-b208-917f67eaa2a5" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512873 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=6.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))=-2(6)=-12\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}=3(1)^{2}=3.\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2f(x)}{3{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(-2f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{3} \\\\  & =-4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512765 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512874 1069 "q-256797" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512874 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=(1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512874 1072 #uuid "12761a27-c492-58e4-90c1-fe69f195f607" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512874 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =((1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(-1-2x(1-x)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =((2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(4x-2-2x(2{x}^{2}-2x-1)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(2{x}_{n}^{2}-2{x}_{n}-1){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}-2){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(2{x}_{0}^{2}-2{x}_{0}-1){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{0}^{3}+4{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}-2){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(2(1.5)^{2}-2(1.5)-1){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}}{(-4(1.5)^{3}+4(1.5)^{2}+6(1.5)-2){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512783 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512875 1069 "q-152022" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512875 1071 "Fred picked $10$ watermelons from his vegetable garden. The weight of watermelons he grows is normally distributed with a mean of $11\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $3\\,\\text{kg}.$ What is the probability that the total weight of the watermelons Fred picked is greater than $100\\,\\text{kg}$ and less than $110\\,\\text{kg}?$ You may assume that the weights of the watermelons are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ |" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512875 1072 #uuid "141fc269-b2bd-5ee7-86fd-0b448fecc7f6" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512875 1074 "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th watermelon. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(11,{3}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 10.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denotes the total weight of the $10$ watermelons. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =10\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =110, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =10\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(110,90).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(100<W<110),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<W<110) & =P(\\frac{100-110}{\\sqrt{90}}<Z<\\frac{110-110}{\\sqrt{90}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.05<Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-1.05) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-(1-Φ(1.05)) \\\\  & =0.5-(1-Φ(1.05)) \\\\  & =Φ(1.05)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.05)≈0.8531.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<W<110) & ≈Φ(1.05)-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.3531.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512762 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512876 1069 "q-293604" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512876 1071 "A generator consumes $3$ liters of fuel per hour for the first two hours of use and $5$ liters for each additional hour. Which function $F$ gives the total amount of fuel consumed after $t$ hours, where $t\\ge 2?$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512876 1072 #uuid "18899ab8-1919-5955-a40e-8e20033f8007" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512876 1074 "The total amount of fuel consumed consists of two parts.\n\n- For the first $2$ hours, the generator consumes\n\n$$\n3\\times 2=6\\,\\,\\text{liters}.\n$$\n\n- For each additional hour beyond the first $2,$ the generator consumes\n\n$$\n5(t-2)\\,\\,\\text{liters}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total amount of fuel $F$ consumed after $t$ hours, where $t\\ge 2,$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =6+5(t-2) \\\\  & =6+5t-10 \\\\  & =5t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512808 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512877 1069 "q-288661" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512877 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\n(ax-8)(x+b)=4{x}^{2}-32x+48\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants. What is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$a+b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512877 1072 #uuid "19e31d5d-5933-52fe-a699-d3240dcd05a2" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512877 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the product on the left-hand side must expand to match the right-hand side.\n\nFirst, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(ax-8)(x+b) & =a{x}^{2}+abx-8x-8b \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}+(ab-8)x-8b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we equate the expanded form to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+(ab-8)x-8b=4{x}^{2}-32x+48.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms.\n\n- Equating the coefficients of ${x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\na=4.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n-8\\cdot b=48⇒b=-6.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that values $a=4$ and $b=-6$ produce the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ab-8 & =4\\cdot (-6)-8 \\\\  & =-24-8 \\\\  & =-32\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the right-hand side. So, our solution is valid.\n\nFinally, we compute the value of $a+b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =4+(-6) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512753 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512878 1069 "q-140469" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512878 1071 "Find ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512878 1072 #uuid "1b5b5791-1037-533d-a2f0-5ffb4bac0dd8" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512878 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-14{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -14 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512835 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512879 1069 "q-342898" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512879 1071 "![](images/19/192a69596e851fa35b02bbfd6bda06f01814d0d1c05b5698a4179d00a4bb14d2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512879 1072 #uuid "1ef4f411-6c67-5c13-837f-d11ff722cfe0" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512879 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-8) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,-10\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512728 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512880 1069 "q-147950" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512880 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}({x}^{3}+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{14}{25}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{19}{15},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512880 1072 #uuid "28314126-6eaa-5326-a420-9e962fb3a315" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512880 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}({x}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{25}y+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{25}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{15}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}-(\\frac{14}{25})(\\frac{19}{15}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512722 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512881 1069 "q-334747" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512881 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512881 1072 #uuid "288ecca2-4a65-5e47-af70-06519e1d4ea1" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512881 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512739 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512882 1069 "q-147968" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512882 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.65$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512882 1072 #uuid "2ce015c9-6f9e-5825-a2d1-d00ea38bdb08" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512882 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.65$ | $0.65$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.1$ | $0.9$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.35+2\\cdot 0.65 \\\\  & =0+1.3 \\\\  & =1.3 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.1+4\\cdot 0.9 \\\\  & =0.1+3.6 \\\\  & =3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.3)(y-3.7)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0-1.3)(1-3.7)f(0,1) \\\\  & +(0-1.3)(4-3.7)f(0,4) \\\\  & +(2-1.3)(1-3.7)f(2,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.3)(4-3.7)f(2,4) \\\\  & =(-1.3)(-2.7)(0.1) \\\\  & +(-1.3)(0.3)(0.25) \\\\  & +(0.7)(-2.7)(0) \\\\  & +(0.7)(0.3)(0.65) \\\\  & =0.39.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512855 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512883 1069 "q-162896" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512883 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+4z=4$ and the cylinder $4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512883 1072 #uuid "3250257a-998e-56c2-b2b7-fdd8d8e6863b" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512883 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+4z=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,4⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 1=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=4\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\mu x=0 \\\\ \\lambda =1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the first equation above, we must have $x=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(1) & =4 \\\\ y+4 & =4 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=0$ and $z=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+4z & =4 \\\\ 0+y+4(-1) & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =4 \\\\ y & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0,1),\\,(0,8,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(0,0,1)$ | $(0,8,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,8,-1),$ and its  maximum value is $10.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512800 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512884 1069 "q-367477" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512884 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.2$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512884 1072 #uuid "34cbee90-4c9c-598a-b567-3653b89bdcd4" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512884 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4)^{′}={e}^{x}-12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-12{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-12{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{{e}^{1.2}-6(1.2)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.2}-12(1.2)} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-12{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875…-\\frac{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-6(1.080\\,875…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-12(1.080\\,875…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={x}_{2}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6{x}_{2}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-12{x}_{2}} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…-\\frac{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-6(1.074\\,640…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-12(1.074\\,640…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,623….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.07$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.07$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.07,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512828 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512885 1069 "q-251893" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512885 1071 "Consider two sets of $28$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{28},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{28}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{28}{x}_{i}=560,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{28}{y}_{i}=588, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{28}{x}_{i}^{2}=10\\,920,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{28}{y}_{i}^{2}=13\\,220.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the sum of the squared deviations from the mean for the points ${y}_{i}.$\n\n${S}_{yy}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512885 1072 #uuid "42fccfda-10de-5970-a9ed-3564137d6e6d" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512885 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${y}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & =13\\,220-\\frac{1}{28}(588)^{2} \\\\  & =13\\,220-12\\,348 \\\\  & =872.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512767 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512782 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512806 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512811 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512886 1069 "q-332402" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512886 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{6}{s}-\\frac{2}{s-4},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{4t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512886 1072 #uuid "4d0a8858-fa20-5f8a-859e-5ff864dcdd44" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512886 1074 "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{6}{s}-\\frac{2}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =6{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}-2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1-2{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & =6-2{e}^{4t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =3{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & =3+{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6-2{e}^{4t} \\\\ 3+{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512747 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512887 1069 "q-138609" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512887 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 & 15 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512887 1072 #uuid "52c2be5d-881c-537a-9b60-cd461ebff8ca" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512887 1074 "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}39 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512848 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512888 1069 "q-147945" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512888 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{13}{10}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{13}{10},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512888 1072 #uuid "57775fd5-4d0c-5406-bb3e-6af6db58e9c9" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512888 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y[\\frac{32}{5}+2{y}^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5}y+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{40}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}-(\\frac{13}{10})(\\frac{13}{10}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{100}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512792 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512889 1069 "q-112756" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512889 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=a{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+bx+1$ has a factor of $(x-1)$ and has a remainder of $4$ when divided by $(x+2).$ Find the value of $a-b.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512889 1072 #uuid "5db961df-fac4-59ee-a59e-6595583c0931" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512889 1074 "Since $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, it must divide into $f(x)$ with no remainder. In other words, when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-1),$ the remainder is $0.$ It follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =f(1) \\\\  & =a(1)^{3}-2(1)^{2}+b(1)+1 \\\\  & =a-2+b+1 \\\\  & =a+b-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since we're told that the remainder is $4$ when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2),$ it follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =f(-2) \\\\  & =a(-2)^{3}-2(-2)^{2}+b(-2)+1 \\\\  & =-8a-8-2b+1 \\\\  & =-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=a+b-1 \\\\ 4=-8a-2b-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying each equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+b=1 \\\\ 8a+2b=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $b,$ we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ and we add the resulting equation to the second equation. So we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2a-2b=-2 \\\\ +\\,\\,\\, & \\,8a+2b=-11 \\\\  & \\,6a=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $a=-\\frac{13}{6}.$ Finally, we substitute it into $a+b=1$ to find $b=\\frac{19}{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, our final solution is $a-b=-\\frac{16}{3}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512737 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512890 1069 "q-251855" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512890 1071 "Consider two sets of $18$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{18},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{18}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=36,\\,{S}_{yy}=27,\\,{S}_{xy}=30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, ${\\sigma }_{y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512890 1072 #uuid "5f1ce794-6e38-5896-94a0-a08e42af5009" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512890 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the variance of ${y}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{y}^{2} & =\\frac{{S}_{yy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{yy}$ is the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${y}_{i}.$\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{yy}=27.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{y}^{2} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512856 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512891 1069 "q-66857" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512891 1071 "$\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{e})=$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512891 1072 #uuid "5f3a9a12-37bd-5534-bce9-24153ef1f5e3" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512891 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[4]{e}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{e}={e}^{1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{e})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/4})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{1/4})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{8}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512861 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512892 1069 "q-3414" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512892 1071 "Caden sold half of his comic books and then bought sixteen more. If he now has $36$, how many comic books did he have at the start?" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512892 1072 #uuid "671acf01-53e4-5ebe-ba29-cc75ccac1d67" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512892 1074 "Let $x=$ the number of comic books Caden had at the start.\n\nWe note that:\n\n- Half the number of comic books corresponds to $\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n- The number of comic books remaining after selling half of them corresponds to $x-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n- The number of comic books remaining after selling half and buying sixteen more corresponds to $x-\\frac{1}{2}x+16.$\n\nThe final number of comic books is $36,$ so we have the equation\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{1}{2}x+16=36.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{1}{2}x+16 & =36 \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2})x+16 & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+16 & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+16-16 & =36-16 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =20 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\ x & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, Caden had $40$ comic books at the start." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512749 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512893 1069 "q-138640" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512893 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512893 1072 #uuid "67636a56-1e5b-5200-b7f3-14f05ab714df" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512893 1074 "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512846 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512894 1069 "q-256818" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512894 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2x+3$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512894 1072 #uuid "69e00eda-cc65-5659-9307-298376017360" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512894 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2x+3)^{′}=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x-2, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x-2)^{′}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{n})+{x}_{n}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{n})+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{0})+{x}_{0}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{0})+3} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+2-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3} \\\\  & =1.367\\,897…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{2{x}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{1}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+3} \\\\  & =(1.367\\,897…)-\\frac{2(1.367\\,897…)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.367\\,897…)+(1.367\\,897…)-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.367\\,897…)+3} \\\\  & ≈1.306,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512778 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512895 1069 "q-76442" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512895 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\n Find $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=t+2.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512895 1072 #uuid "6a15f1db-7af4-5426-9f16-816905676768" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512895 1074 "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+0+4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512815 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512896 1069 "q-256788" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512896 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x}$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512896 1072 #uuid "70144f61-d6d7-503d-8fc3-74d38b71f718" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512896 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-2x+2-(-{x}^{2}+2x+1)){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4x+1){e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(-{x}_{n}^{2}+2{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}{({x}_{n}^{2}-4{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(-{x}_{0}^{2}+2{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}}{({x}_{0}^{2}-4{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(-(1.5)^{2}+2(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}}{((1.5)^{2}-4(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}} \\\\  & ≈2.136,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512851 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512897 1069 "q-147971" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512897 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512897 1072 #uuid "72eb6c64-1cf3-5dde-b025-69a7158cf18b" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512897 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.65$ | $0.35$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0+1.2 \\\\  & =1.2 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.65+3\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =0.65+1.05 \\\\  & =1.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.2)(y-1.7)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0-1.2)(1-1.7)f(0,1) \\\\  & +(0-1.2)(3-1.7)f(0,3) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(1-1.7)f(2,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(3-1.7)f(2,3) \\\\  & =(-1.2)(-0.7)(0.15) \\\\  & +(-1.2)(1.3)(0.25) \\\\  & +(0.8)(-0.7)(0.5) \\\\  & +(0.8)(1.3)(0.1) \\\\  & =-0.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512763 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512898 1069 "q-118876" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512898 1071 "A company rents ten identical storage units in the form of a rectangular prism. Each unit is $5\\,\\text{yd}$ long, $3\\,\\text{yd}$ wide, and $3\\text{yd}$ high. If it costs $$2$ per cubic yard per day to rent a storage unit, how much does it cost the company each day to rent the storage units?" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512898 1072 #uuid "751e0aa2-a931-559b-9a2f-5250360d1346" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512898 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of one storage unit.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=5,\\,w=3,\\,h=3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=5\\cdot 3\\cdot 3=45\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total volume of the ten units is\n\n$$\n45\\cdot 10=450\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to rent the storage units, each day the company must pay\n\n$$\n450\\cdot $2=$900.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512735 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512899 1069 "q-147925" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512899 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.12$ | $0.28$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.18$ | $0.12$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512899 1072 #uuid "7601220c-590a-55f7-9ebc-4ae99b2759bd" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512899 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.12$ | $0.28$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.18$ | $0.12$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.3$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.5+4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.3+3\\cdot 0.3+5\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.3+0.9+2 \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3)(y-3.2)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512774 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512822 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512900 1069 "q-342372" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512900 1071 "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512900 1072 #uuid "7658d4f4-3b1c-50cc-bb09-9642192fb8ae" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512900 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)-(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1)=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512723 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512901 1069 "q-251862" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512901 1071 "Consider two sets of $16$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{16}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=188,\\,{S}_{yy}=354,\\,{S}_{xy}=212.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512901 1072 #uuid "783f4f6c-a7ae-5188-a502-9fe2f7c3c94b" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512901 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=212.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{212}{16} \\\\  & =13.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512801 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512902 1069 "q-34313" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512902 1071 "![](images/23/23f0dba237502bc7261fd8f28327beefd5872bb4b0f503930c488b2857fcef24.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},-1),$ calculate the angle $\\theta .$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512902 1072 #uuid "7e50efe9-568f-54f5-835e-03a6e09d3518" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512902 1074 "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](images/d1/d14f62179f831ebdc177eac7bd2899679864305d4104d4b61f304eb4fa9e133d.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={210}^{∘}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512743 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512903 1069 "q-334749" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512903 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6}$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512903 1072 #uuid "80296baf-83a9-58bd-9c2b-7e79ee3813aa" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512903 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $3(Δx)^{4}=3(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{5}=7(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512745 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512904 1069 "q-334826" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512904 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.3125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512904 1072 #uuid "87976b3b-af1b-5fcb-8c01-533b581f6a90" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512904 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}=(2.5)^{4}=39.0625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.3125}{39.0625}=0.008.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512780 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512905 1069 "q-147964" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512905 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$\n\n*Hint: It can be shown that $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible values of $x$ and $y.$*" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512905 1072 #uuid "88c40e55-9afb-513d-8399-c6e943bf9ded" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512905 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\nP(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values of $x$ and $y.$ Therefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent. \n\nSince $X$ are $Y$ are independent, we have $\\text{Cov}(X,Y)=0.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512776 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512906 1069 "q-43813" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512906 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nFind $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)=3+t-2{t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t-3.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512906 1072 #uuid "890c5dfd-af10-56f3-b11c-f667a9950308" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512906 1074 "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1)+x(2)y(2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-9+0+3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512795 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512907 1069 "q-251895" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512907 1071 "Consider the two sets of $5$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $5$ | $7$ | $8$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $19$ | $25$ | $15$ | $20$ | $19$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}^{2}=255,$ calculate ${S}_{xx}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512907 1072 #uuid "8985aab7-37e2-5eaf-8405-71a24796c1c6" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512907 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ data:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}=5+7+8+6+9=35.\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}^{2}=255.$\n\nFinally, by substituting into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=255-\\frac{1}{5}(35)^{2}=255-245=10.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512741 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512908 1069 "q-112414" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512908 1071 "Find the solution $(a,b)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $a:$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+4b=7 \\\\ -2a+6b=20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512908 1072 #uuid "8b8bf0b2-6f55-5107-9e2d-cc11b6142890" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512908 1074 "Notice that the $a$-coefficient in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5,$ then the $a$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $10$ and $-10,$ and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,5a+4b=7\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ (\\,-2a+6b=20\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10a+8b=14 \\\\ -10a+30b=100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{10a}+8b & =14 \\\\ -\\cancel{10a}+30b & =100 \\\\ (8b+30b) & =(14+100) \\\\ 38b & =114 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $b=3.$ However, we still need to find $a.$ To do this, we substitute $b=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a+4b & =7 \\\\ 5a+4(3) & =7 \\\\ 5a+12 & =7 \\\\ 5a & =-5 \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-1$ and $b=3,$ which can be written as $(-1,3).$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512725 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512909 1069 "q-335084" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512909 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512909 1072 #uuid "8c12baec-9a09-54f7-8159-4ab5ea602070" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512909 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x(0) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512731 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512910 1069 "q-251803" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512910 1071 "Consider the two sets of $7$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $9$ | $11$ | $12$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $3$ | $9$ | $27$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $1$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=494,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512910 1072 #uuid "8f3d0803-8d23-5ea9-98da-36f1c313a55f" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512910 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =9+11+12+4+3+2+8=49, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =3+9+27+2+4+8+1=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=494.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=494-\\frac{1}{7}(49)(54)=494-378=116.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512758 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512911 1069 "q-251797" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512911 1071 "Consider two sets of $36$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{36},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{36}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{36}{x}_{i}=202, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{36}{y}_{i}=225, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{36}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=1\\,329.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,  ${S}_{xy}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512911 1072 #uuid "91b1cdc3-3a29-5654-81e4-e52f93ca5959" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512911 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the statistic ${S}_{xy}$ be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & =1\\,329-\\frac{1}{36}(202)(225) \\\\  & =1\\,329-1\\,262.5 \\\\  & =66.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512777 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512912 1069 "q-148027" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512912 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=1.3$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.9,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512912 1072 #uuid "9576e0da-b7f3-5620-ad1a-43d5569461f3" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512912 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0)(1)(0.15)+(0)(3)(0.2)+(2)(1)(0.4)+(2)(3)(0.25) \\\\  & =2.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =2.3-(1.3)(1.9) \\\\  & =-0.17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512752 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512913 1069 "q-152019" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512913 1071 "In a particular shop, cables are sold in coils. The length of the cable in a coil is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\text{m}$ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{m}.$ If John bought $5$ coils, what is the probability that the total length of the cables he bought is greater than $250\\,\\text{m}$ and less than $253\\,m?$ You may assume that the lengths of the cable in the coils are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9554\\,$ | $\\,0.9564\\,$ | $\\,0.9573\\,$ | $\\,0.9582\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9641\\,$ | $\\,0.9649\\,$ | $\\,0.9656\\,$ | $\\,0.9664\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ |" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512913 1072 #uuid "989181af-177e-5503-a508-c854844640ac" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512913 1074 "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the length of cable in the $i$th coil. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(50,{0.7}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 5.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $L$ denote the total length of the cables in $5$ randomly selected coils. Then, since\n\n$$\nL={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{5},\n$$\n\nwe have that $L$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[L] & =5\\cdot 50 \\\\  & =250, \\\\ \\text{Var}[L] & =5\\cdot {0.7}^{2} \\\\  & =2.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL∼N(250,2.45).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(250<L<253),$ we convert $L$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(250<L<253) & =P(\\frac{250-250}{\\sqrt{2.45}}<Z<\\frac{253-250}{\\sqrt{2.45}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<1.92) \\\\  & =Φ(1.92)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.92)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.92)≈0.9726.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(250<L<253) & ≈Φ(0.95)-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.4726.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512847 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512914 1069 "q-251841" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512914 1071 "Consider the two sets of $7$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $2$ | $3$ | $10$ | $9$ | $6$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $15$ | $21$ | $22$ | $23$ | $26$ | $17$ | $16$ |\n\nCalculate ${S}_{xx}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512914 1072 #uuid "a2d81c89-6939-51ba-b7c7-704655922181" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512914 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ ${S}_{xx}$ is the sum of the squared deviations of the data ${x}_{i}$ from the mean $\\overset{―}{x},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sample mean $\\overset{―}{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{2+3+10+9+6+5+7}{7}=6\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the differences between the ${x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{x}$, and their squares.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $2$ | $3$ | $10$ | $9$ | $6$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $15$ | $21$ | $22$ | $23$ | $26$ | $17$ | $16$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\,$** | $-4$ | $-3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\\,$** | $16$ | $9$ | $16$ | $9$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, ${S}_{xx}$ is\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}=16+9+16+9+0+1+1=52.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512821 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512915 1069 "q-138641" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512915 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 13 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512915 1072 #uuid "a2f76723-41c1-52ba-a0e5-e2eb590f9ddf" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512915 1074 "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512788 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512916 1069 "q-256783" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512916 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x-5$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.8.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512916 1072 #uuid "a34954f0-edee-5e78-b8c6-4b95b31dcf9d" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512916 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x-5)^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-4x+1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{3}-4x+1)^{′} \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =4(3{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-4{x}_{n}+1}{4(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{3}-4{x}_{0}+1}{4(3{x}_{0}^{2}-1)} \\\\  & =1.8-\\frac{4(1.8)^{3}-4(1.8)+1}{4(3(1.8)^{2}-1)} \\\\  & ≈1.309,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512789 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512917 1069 "q-66526" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512917 1071 "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512917 1072 #uuid "a5359436-369e-5935-88c6-73a8053a9364" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512917 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{1/2}}={e}^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512775 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512918 1069 "q-147967" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512918 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.5$ | $0$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512918 1072 #uuid "a725de9b-9a65-5d96-80a3-b82e15275c01" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512918 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+3\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5+1.5 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.7+4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =1.4+1.2 \\\\  & =2.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2)(y-2.6)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1-2)(2-2.6)f(1,2) \\\\  & +(1-2)(4-2.6)f(1,4) \\\\  & +(3-2)(2-2.6)f(3,2) \\\\  & +(3-2)(4-2.6)f(3,4) \\\\  & =(-1)(-0.6)(0.5) \\\\  & +(-1)(1.4)(0) \\\\  & +(1)(-0.6)(0.2) \\\\  & +(1)(1.4)(0.3) \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512793 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512919 1069 "q-341256" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512919 1071 "![](images/91/91441cecdbe477e4cf0115c1f7c4cf413fa36786690ff7e025ac2e62c711f66e.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ if $h(-2)=6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512919 1072 #uuid "a823921c-a56b-531a-8f8b-ef8538df5571" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512919 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-2,4],$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/19/1996a5ab1052a63a9513ed57a6a74ea82ad5994b62288cb04e4df02dac7589b6.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-2) & =6 \\\\ h(0) & =h(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(3) & =h(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8+3 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ h(4) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11+(-1) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $h$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{6,8,11,10\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{min}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512852 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512920 1069 "q-251853" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512920 1071 "Consider two sets of $36$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{36},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{36}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=72,\\,{S}_{yy}=36,\\,{S}_{xy}=54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, ${\\sigma }_{x}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512920 1072 #uuid "ac49558f-18ee-5f4d-ae88-d48f8fce7444" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512920 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the variance of ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{x}^{2} & =\\frac{{S}_{xx}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xx}$ is the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xx}=72.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{x}^{2} & =\\frac{72}{36} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512827 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512921 1069 "q-251795" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512921 1071 "Consider two sets of $16$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{16}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}=168, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{y}_{i}=158, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=1\\,742.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, ${S}_{xy}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512921 1072 #uuid "ae961e11-0326-56d9-bf54-b78685d8bc1a" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512921 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the statistic ${S}_{xy}$ be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & =1\\,742-\\frac{1}{16}(168)(158) \\\\  & =1\\,742-1\\,659 \\\\  & =83.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512842 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512922 1069 "q-104255" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512922 1071 "![](images/0c/0c23b2d07d656695feda6ae6e92d192661e30f2738b6af8342813b44f2885667.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $6,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=1.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512922 1072 #uuid "aeb0f117-1019-5572-b78f-39043f8c3ded" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512922 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,6,0\\}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=0,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512820 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512923 1069 "q-251859" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512923 1071 "Consider two sets of $10$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{10}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx}=5,\\,{S}_{yy}=7,\\,{S}_{xy}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, $Cov(x,y)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512923 1072 #uuid "af73b3f9-9505-5255-a05e-b12a6848bab1" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512923 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ their covariance can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their means.\n\nWe are given that ${S}_{xy}=6.$ Substituting the given statistic in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cov(x,y) & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512809 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512924 1069 "q-367472" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512924 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=0.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512924 1072 #uuid "afb3acd5-a2e1-5607-b3f9-fd70e360b05d" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512924 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x)^{′}=5{x}^{4}+6x-1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}+6x-1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}+6)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1)}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.5)^{4}+1}{20(0.5)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.227\\,941….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.227\\,941…)^{4}+1}{20(0.227\\,941…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,829…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{2}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.166\\,829…)^{4}+1}{20(0.166\\,829…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,033….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.17$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.17$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈0.17,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512799 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512925 1069 "q-256804" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512925 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{1}{x+1}$  in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=0.8.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512925 1072 #uuid "b0787255-f508-561f-a5f7-88e7552739fb" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512925 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}+2}-\\frac{1}{({x}_{n}+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{({x}_{n}+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{({x}_{n}+1)^{3}})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{0}+2}-\\frac{1}{({x}_{0}+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{({x}_{0}+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{({x}_{0}+1)^{3}})} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{0.8+2}-\\frac{1}{(0.8+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{(0.8+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(0.8+1)^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈0.575,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512837 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512926 1069 "q-147809" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512926 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512926 1072 #uuid "b1903a33-4088-5de3-962d-d8462d93f10a" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512926 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.5+1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0+0.5 \\\\  & =0.5 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.4+1.2 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-0.5)(y-1.6)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512853 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512927 1069 "q-138591" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512927 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512927 1072 #uuid "b27cf292-094d-512e-855d-8e418541cd09" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512927 1074 "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512724 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512928 1069 "q-148030" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512928 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=4$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.4,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512928 1072 #uuid "b781bcca-e045-541a-9fb8-bd56d31a763e" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512928 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(1)(0.2)+(2)(2)(0.1)+(4)(1)(0.2) \\\\  & +(4)(2)(0.2)+(6)(1)(0.2)+(6)(2)(0.1) \\\\  & =5.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =5.6-(4)(1.4) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512787 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512929 1069 "q-147386" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512929 1071 "A fitness center has $1600$ members, $480$ of which play tennis. If the center randomly samples $60$ members, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9418\\,$ | $\\,0.9429\\,$ | $\\,0.9441\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ | $\\,0.9535\\,$ | $\\,0.9545\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ | $\\,0.9625\\,$ | $\\,0.9633\\,$ |" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512929 1072 #uuid "b7b25e27-3f05-5502-a400-9d84bc6b903e" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512929 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=60,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{480}{1600}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=60(0.3)=18>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=60(1-0.3)=42>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=60$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & >\\frac{24}{60}=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.69)=0.9545.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}) & =1-Φ(1.69) \\\\  & =0.0455.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512770 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512813 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512930 1069 "q-343834" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512930 1071 "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512930 1072 #uuid "b98de4ab-a079-5d39-b165-ba7998caa236" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512930 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512730 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512931 1069 "q-162817" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512931 1071 "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=3xz+{y}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x-y=1$ and $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2y,\\,3x⟩=\\lambda ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ x-y=1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512931 1072 #uuid "b9abfcd2-1f16-5a00-8346-efd5d81231cb" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512931 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3xz+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3z,\\,2y,\\,3x⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,4z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512805 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512932 1069 "q-146974" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512932 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=88$ from a population where $44%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $52%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512932 1072 #uuid "ba83e244-060a-5dec-904e-55d3bc3fab0a" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512932 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=88,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.44.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=88(0.44)=38.72>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=88(1-0.44)=49.28>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=88$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,\\frac{0.44(1-0.44)}{88}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,0.0028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.52-0.44}{\\sqrt{0.0028}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.51) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.51) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512768 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512933 1069 "q-138607" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512933 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512933 1072 #uuid "c1c1c9b0-94ce-5a76-9444-4ee707ea7768" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512933 1074 "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512810 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512934 1069 "q-138590" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512934 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 7 & -9 \\\\ 4 & 11 & -5 & -14\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512934 1072 #uuid "c1c74464-e8b7-5891-8564-303d0ba39aac" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512934 1074 "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512833 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512935 1069 "q-367494" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512935 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-3{x}^{2}$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,0.5],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=0.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512935 1072 #uuid "c2a1716c-abcd-5c70-b5af-eb300c75e232" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512935 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-3{x}^{2})^{′}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-6x=2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3x), \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-6x)^{′}=-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-6=-2(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-3{x}_{n})}{-2(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+3)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-3{x}_{n}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{0})-3{x}_{0}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{0})+3} \\\\  & =0.5+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.5)-3\\cdot 0.5}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.5)+3} \\\\  & =0.272\\,999….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{1})-3{x}_{1}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{1})+3} \\\\  & =0.272\\,999…+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.272\\,999…)-3\\cdot 0.272\\,999…}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.272\\,999…)+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,140…., \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={x}_{2}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{2})-3{x}_{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{2})+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,140…+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.283\\,140…)-3\\cdot 0.283\\,140…}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.283\\,140…)+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,068….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.28$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.28$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈0.28,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512859 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512936 1069 "q-164407" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512936 1071 "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\nz=1+(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512936 1072 #uuid "c44a3fc0-0b84-5773-98c9-aa672df4ee82" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512936 1074 "Recall that the equation of an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand an elliptic paraboloid centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}},\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is an elliptic paraboloid with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,2,1),\\,a=1,\\,b=3.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512736 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512937 1069 "q-110256" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512937 1071 "A seller delivers bricks on pallets. One pallet contains $500$ bricks. The weight of a brick is normally distributed with a mean of $3\\,\\text{lb}$ and a standard deviation of $0.25\\,\\text{lb},$ while the weight of a pallet is normally distributed with a mean of $40\\,\\text{lb}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{lb}.$ If one pallet with bricks is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs less than $1\\,545\\,\\text{lb}?$ You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512937 1072 #uuid "c6ec9582-211a-54f7-b78a-6175feef090c" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512937 1074 "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th brick, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a pallet. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(3,{0.25}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 500,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(40,{4}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected pallet with bricks. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{500}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =500\\cdot 3+40 \\\\  & =1\\,540, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =500\\cdot {0.25}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =47.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,540,47.25).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<1\\,545),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & =P(Z<\\frac{1\\,545-1\\,540}{\\sqrt{47.25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.73) \\\\  & =Φ(0.73)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.73)≈0.7673.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & ≈Φ(0.73) \\\\  & ≈0.7673.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512830 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512938 1069 "q-85920" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512938 1071 "A team of $5$ players is to be chosen at random from $7$ boys and $5$ girls.  What is the probability that $2$ boys and $3$ girls will be chosen? Round your answer to the nearest percent." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512938 1072 #uuid "c7f9805c-ce96-515d-891a-332594997042" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512938 1074 "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to choose $5$ people out of a total of $12,$ and $n$ is the number of picks where $2$ are boys and $3$ are girls.\n\nSince the order in which the players are chosen does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to pick $5$ people from a total of $12$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{12}{C}_{5} & =\\frac{12!}{5!\\cdot 7!}=792.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $7$) boys is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{2}=\\frac{7!}{2!\\cdot 5!}=21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to choose $3$ (out of $5$) girls is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{3!\\cdot 2!}=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n={}_{7}{C}_{2}\\cdot {}_{5}{C}_{3}=21\\cdot 10=210.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{210}{792}≈0.27=27%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512769 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512939 1069 "q-251804" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512939 1071 "Consider the two sets of $8$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $8$ | $6$ | $10$ | $5$ | $1$ | $12$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $7$ | $1$ | $6$ | $11$ | $6$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=260,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512939 1072 #uuid "c92a6a30-c262-56d7-be68-e946a5d2130d" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512939 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =8+6+10+5+1+12+4+3=49, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =2+4+5+7+1+6+11+6=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=260.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=260-\\frac{1}{8}(49)(42)=260-257.25=2.75.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512757 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512940 1069 "q-256826" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512940 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-2x-1$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512940 1072 #uuid "ce107c95-0185-5fbd-a5eb-aca5a16f428e" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512940 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2x-1)^{′}={e}^{x}-2, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2)^{′}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{0.5}-2}{{e}^{0.5}} \\\\  & =0.713\\,061…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}} \\\\  & =(0.713\\,061…)-\\frac{{e}^{0.713\\,061…}-2}{{e}^{0.713\\,061…}} \\\\  & ≈0.693,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512839 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512941 1069 "q-147844" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512941 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{7}{12}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512941 1072 #uuid "cf31f09e-0f29-56f8-9f97-abccd7a0062d" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512941 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512818 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512942 1069 "q-251899" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512942 1071 "Consider the two sets of $6$ paired observations shown in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $9$ | $10$ | $13$ | $11$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $12$ | $13$ | $16$ | $14$ | $14$ | $15$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{y}_{i}^{2}=1\\,186,$ calculate ${S}_{yy}.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512942 1072 #uuid "d18e628f-0f71-500f-a909-b31e50aba9f8" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512942 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${y}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${y}_{i}$ data:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{y}_{i}=12+13+16+14+14+15=84.\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{y}_{i}^{2}=1\\,186.$\n\nFinally, by substituting into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{yy}=1\\,186-\\frac{1}{6}(84)^{2}=1\\,186-1\\,176=10.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512779 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512943 1069 "q-335072" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512943 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512943 1072 #uuid "d29ce693-46aa-5cb5-a64c-988886b4e7a0" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512943 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512797 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512944 1069 "q-147970" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512944 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512944 1072 #uuid "d4d126ea-cebf-548f-a0ce-ca7561957246" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512944 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.8+1.8 \\\\  & =2.6 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.7+2\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.7+0.6 \\\\  & =1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2.6)(y-1.3)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2-2.6)(1-1.3)f(2,1) \\\\  & +(2-2.6)(2-1.3)f(2,2) \\\\  & +(3-2.6)(1-1.3)f(3,1) \\\\  & +(3-2.6)(2-1.3)f(3,2) \\\\  & =(-0.6)(-0.3)(0.3) \\\\  & +(-0.6)(0.7)(0.1) \\\\  & +(0.4)(-0.3)(0.4) \\\\  & +(0.4)(0.7)(0.2) \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512794 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512945 1069 "q-335070" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512945 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$1-{e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t}$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512945 1072 #uuid "dcde9f99-5a2a-52a5-a122-c3ef3631c507" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512945 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}(-s+t+1){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512838 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512946 1069 "q-148028" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512946 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=3.2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=3.8,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512946 1072 #uuid "de144ac2-d89b-5e69-8e47-de2554ce330e" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512946 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(3)(0.25)+(2)(5)(0.15)+(4)(3)(0.35)+(4)(5)(0.25) \\\\  & =12.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =12.2-(3.2)(3.8) \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512841 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512947 1069 "q-19083" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512947 1071 "![](images/42/429cba100e1cebd4a2048226f3c9d252fdec171b33679ab8ff6ba21014386aa4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=4.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512947 1072 #uuid "de36a313-5e05-5888-a50c-ca20f5e00cf3" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512947 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d2/d20759e0c246f1fad5e0a36e495302c6b8de0e23a5f8ed25bc291147750a1342.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1)+4.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,3,7.5\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512748 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512948 1069 "q-389801" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512948 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{6}-2{x}^{5}+{x}^{2}$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.8$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512948 1072 #uuid "de551d86-8bf0-5d70-b4cf-bdf9b46a682a" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512948 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{6}-2{x}^{5}+{x}^{2})^{′}=6{x}^{5}-10{x}^{4}+2x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{5}-10{x}^{4}+2x)^{′}=30{x}^{4}-40{x}^{3}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{6{x}_{n}^{5}-10{x}_{n}^{4}+2{x}_{n}}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2)-(6{x}_{n}^{5}-10{x}_{n}^{4}+2{x}_{n})}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{x}_{n}^{5}-30{x}_{n}^{4}}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{x}_{n}^{5}-15{x}_{n}^{4}}{15{x}_{n}^{4}-20{x}_{n}^{3}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{12{x}_{0}^{5}-15{x}_{0}^{4}}{15{x}_{0}^{4}-20{x}_{0}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.8)^{5}-15(1.8)^{4}}{15(1.8)^{4}-20(1.8)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.656\\,564….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{12{x}_{1}^{5}-15{x}_{1}^{4}}{15{x}_{1}^{4}-20{x}_{1}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.656\\,564…)^{5}-15(1.656\\,564…)^{4}}{15(1.656\\,564…)^{4}-20(1.656\\,564…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.594\\,573…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{12{x}_{2}^{5}-15{x}_{2}^{4}}{15{x}_{2}^{4}-20{x}_{2}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.594\\,573…)^{5}-15(1.594\\,573…)^{4}}{15(1.594\\,573…)^{4}-20(1.594\\,573…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.582\\,950…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{12{x}_{3}^{5}-15{x}_{3}^{4}}{15{x}_{3}^{4}-20{x}_{3}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.582\\,950…)^{5}-15(1.582\\,950…)^{4}}{15(1.582\\,950…)^{4}-20(1.582\\,950…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.582\\,567….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.58$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.58$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.58,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512784 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512949 1069 "q-331948" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512949 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-9)}{(s-1)(s-4)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512949 1072 #uuid "e1726d67-859b-50b6-a736-a3033ae62d0e" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512949 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4=2{X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3=-{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s-2){X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)=4 \\\\ {X}_{1}(s)+(s-3){X}_{2}(s)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=3-(s-3){X}_{2}(s).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-2)[3-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)]+2{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ 3(s-2)-[(s-2)(s-3)-2]{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ -({s}^{2}-5s+4){X}_{2}(s) & =4-3(s-2) \\\\ -(s-1)(s-4){X}_{2}(s) & =-3s+10 \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =3-(s-3){X}_{2}(s) \\\\  & =3-(s-3)\\cdot \\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s-1)(s-4)-(s-3)(3s-10)}{(s-1)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s-18}{(s-1)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2s-9)}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-9)}{(s-1)(s-4)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512798 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512950 1069 "q-342899" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512950 1071 "![](images/98/98290071482fa2a8082932304fbb0d0cc0db54c47324479da7979079e9542551.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $9$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,3]$ given that $f(-4)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512950 1072 #uuid "e2f10b5f-3a80-55aa-94de-01383831523e" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512950 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6-9 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,5,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512733 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512951 1069 "q-334642" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512951 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.24.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.02.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512951 1072 #uuid "ebc091be-da12-5892-8a06-6dc1bcb20797" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512951 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.24$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.24}{0.02}=12.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈12 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(12) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.585… \\\\ p & ≈2.585….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512819 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512952 1069 "q-147922" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512952 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512952 1072 #uuid "ef29076a-1f6b-53fd-9d0b-390107f2fcd7" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512952 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.55+6\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5+2.2+1.2 \\\\  & =3.9 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.8+2.4 \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3.9)(y-3.2)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512857 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512953 1069 "q-342944" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512953 1071 "![](images/ab/ab8e0cbaf83156609db79ec08202c8f77acfebd03663dfe273e4d75f9db03afb.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ if $f(0)=4.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512953 1072 #uuid "f237f691-54ad-5d16-bd80-dc2031f08456" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512953 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2,4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ea/ea837252a62313eae506ef3d101c0b70121f6380694a49c2f54e1a19702949a5.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3) \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3)+0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,5,2,2.5\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512802 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512954 1069 "q-138597" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512954 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512954 1072 #uuid "f38b0380-d78d-5094-b9f0-211071b581d6" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512954 1074 "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512734 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512955 1069 "q-251891" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512955 1071 "Consider two sets of $20$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{20},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{20}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}=400,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{y}_{i}=440, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}^{2}=8\\,590,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{y}_{i}^{2}=9\\,740.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the sum of the squared deviations from the mean for the points ${x}_{i}.$\n\n${S}_{xx}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512955 1072 #uuid "f69f9435-629a-5550-ae7c-8e46c05363dd" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512955 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & =8\\,590-\\frac{1}{20}(400)^{2} \\\\  & =8\\,590-8\\,000 \\\\  & =590.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512744 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512956 1069 "q-256825" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512956 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512956 1072 #uuid "f6e563d1-d5b4-5654-a9e3-927ced1c53e0" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512956 1074 "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-24x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-24{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{5{x}_{0}^{4}-12{x}_{0}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-24{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{5(2)^{4}-12(2)^{2}+1}{20(2)^{3}-24(2)} \\\\  & =1.705\\,357…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{5{x}_{1}^{4}-12{x}_{1}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-24{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(1.705\\,357…)-\\frac{5(1.705\\,357…)^{4}-12(1.705\\,357…)^{2}+1}{20(1.705\\,357…)^{3}-24(1.705\\,357…)} \\\\  & ≈1.561,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512729 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512756 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512771 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512834 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512957 1069 "q-332299" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512957 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s-1},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{2}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{4t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512957 1072 #uuid "fafb126e-2901-5759-82dc-9622ce8bea50" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512957 1074 "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & =3\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & =3{e}^{1\\cdot t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{2}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\}-2\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot t}-2{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}-2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512807 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512958 1069 "q-138613" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512958 1071 "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 23 & 16 \\\\ 14 & 20 & 20\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512958 1072 #uuid "fd960ab2-8677-5176-9a5c-025cfe279786" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512958 1074 "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ 54\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512785 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512959 1069 "q-148014" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512959 1071 "If $\\text{E}[X]=5.2,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=4.2,$ and $\\text{E}[XY]=22$ for two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512959 1072 #uuid "fefbc176-d8f3-53d4-bb24-386b85087084" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512959 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =22-(5.2)\\cdot (4.2) \\\\  & =0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of $X$ and $Y